Roadmap Narrative

When I look at my roadmap up close the clearest thread is that I am gradually becoming more focused on nuclear engineering and building toward a professional career in the field. My first year was different because I started at community college and was mostly focused on general education courses and figuring out what direction I wanted to take. After deciding on nuclear engineering, the rest of my roadmap becomes much more focused on building the academic knowledge, technical skills, and experience I will need for that career.
The lane that feels the most underdeveloped is professional experience. I did not have many opportunities to gain engineering experience or make connections in the nuclear field last year and spent the majority of my time working in restaurants to save up for school. I want to make up for that by attending engineering career fairs, applying for internships and research positions, and looking into opportunities around Oak Ridge. I also want to use Handshake and talk with professors about opportunities that might help me gain experience earlier.
I think the transition from student to professional really begins during my junior year. That is when I plan to complete a serious internship or research experience related to nuclear engineering. Up until that point, most of my experience will come from classes and school projects. An internship or research position would be significant because I would finally be applying what I have learned to real engineering problems and working with people who are already in the industry. It would also help me understand what type of nuclear engineering work I am most interested in.
This roadmap shows me that I am becoming more independent and serious about my future. My path has not been completely direct, especially because I started at community college and did not accomplish everything I might have wanted during my first year. However, that also shows that my plan can change and still move forward. I am becoming someone who is willing to adjust, work toward a more specific goal, and take advantage of opportunities as they become available. By the end of the four years, I want to be prepared not only to graduate with a nuclear engineering degree, but also to confidently begin a professional career.
